# --- s008 Book I d.1 q.1 (= Ord. I d.1 p.1 q.1, nn. 1–22)
n("n211","s008-h","[Liber primus. Distinctio prima. Questio prima.]","la","textual","Editorial heading. A has no heading: the question opens with a large red *C* (*Irca*), and the running headline *Di.i.* starts on p. 17. This is where the Prologue ends and Gorris’s commentary on Book I begins.")
n("n212","s008-01","83. questionum q. 36.","la","source","Augustine, *De diversis quaestionibus 83*, q. 30 (*fruendum est … bonis invisibilibus*). Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 1 n. 1, has *quaestione 30*, so A’s *36* is a misprint or misreading. B not checked here.","verified")
n("n213","s008-01","de doctrina christiana. c. p°.","la","source","Augustine, *De doctrina christiana* I.5.5: *Res igitur quibus fruendum est, Pater et Filius et Spiritus Sanctus, eademque Trinitas, una quaedam summa res*. This is the *ad oppositum* of *Ord.* I d. 1 p. 1 q. 1.","verified")
n("n214","s008-02","Auicenne .9. metha. c. 4.","la","source","Avicenna, *Metaph.* IX c. 4 (the emanation of the intelligences; each intelligence finds rest in what is above it). Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 1 n. 9: *videtur esse opinio Avicennae quod fruitio ordinata potest esse circa aliud a fine ultimo*. Gorris supplies the chapter.","verified")
n("n215","s008-02","Boneuenture li. 1. di. 1. articulo .2°. q. 3.","la","source","Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* d. 1 (the argument from the *imago Dei*, which is *capax Dei* and therefore satisfied by God alone). B reads *ar. xx.* (?) *q. iij.* The usual reference is d. 1 a. 3 q. 2 (*utrum solo Deo sit fruendum*), so the article number is doubtful in both prints.","to-verify")
n("n216","s008-02","beati Thome prima parte summe. q. 12. articulo .1.","la","source","Thomas, *ST* I q. 12 a. 1 (the created intellect can see God’s essence; otherwise natural desire would be vain). The argument that the soul, created immediately by God, can be beatified only by him is cited by Scotus among the *alii* against Avicenna (*Ord.* I d. 1 nn. 10–12).","certain")
n("n217","s008-02","nisi in solo deo","la","textual","B: *nec in solo deo*, an easy minim error. A is right.","verified")
n("n218","s008-03","assistendum voluntati","la","textual","B: *assistendum per voluntatem*, parallel to the preceding *per intellectum*.","verified")
n("n219","s008-04","proportio vitalis","la","source","On proportion, not likeness, between power and object, so that a finite capacity can be ordered to an infinite object, cf. *Ord.* I d. 1 n. 17 ff.: *inter potentiam et obiectum non est relatio similitudinis sed proportionis*.","verified")
n("n220","s008-05","secundum Ambro.","la","source","The fourfold division of the virtues by function (regulating, restraining, intensifying, both) is attributed to Ambrose, but it is not in Ambrose in this form. It resembles the scholastic doctrine of the cardinal virtues (cf. Thomas, *ST* I-II q. 61 a. 2), which is ultimately built on Ambrose, *De officiis* I. The attribution has not been verified.","uncertain")
n("n221","s008-05","5° ethicorum iusticia commutatiua","la","source","Aristotle, *EN* V.2–4, 1130b30 ff. (distributive and corrective/commutative justice). Equating ‘punitive’ justice with commutative justice is Gorris’s (or his source’s) simplification.","certain")
n("n222","s008-06","longe ab homine abest","la","source","Aristotle, *EN* III.11, 1119a6–10: insensibility (the man who delights in nothing) is *far from being human*.","verified")
n("n223","s008-06","que si non est virtus est cum virtute","la","source","Aristotle, *EN* VIII.1, 1155a3–4: friendship *is a virtue or implies virtue*.","verified")
n("n224","s008-05","vti fruendis vel frui vtendis","la","source","Augustine’s classic formula (*De div. qu. 83* q. 30: *omnis perversitas … est uti fruendis et frui utendis*), discussed by Scotus in *Ord.* I d. 1 (q. 3, n. 70). The question which of the two is worse, and its answer by the doctrine of the mean, belong to Gorris.","verified")
n("n225","s008-08","⟨intellexerit⟩","la","textual","A prints *intellerit*, probably for *intellexerit*. The subject is presumably Augustine (the *auctoritas* of the opening argument).","uncertain")
n("n226","s008-01","Eighty-three Questions, q. 36","en","translation","Scotus and Augustine have q. 30; see Latin note.")
n("n227","s008-03","to assent","en","translation","Lat. *assistendum*. The sense requires ‘adhere/assent to’, probably a corruption of *assentiendum*: cf. Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 1 n. 66, on the intellect’s act *quo assentitur alicui vero propter se*.")
n("n228","s008-04","vital proportion","en","translation","*proportio vitalis immutationis*: a proportion of receptive capacity in a living power, not a likeness of nature.")
n("n229","s008-05","to use what is to be enjoyed, or to enjoy what is to be used","en","translation","*uti fruendis vel frui utendis*: Augustine’s technical pair *uti/frui*, kept as ‘use/enjoy’ throughout.")
n("n230","s008-08","[Augustine] will have meant","en","translation","Supplying the subject; the verb form is uncertain in A.")
# --- B collation (full): B19L–B20R
n("n1035","s008-04","appetitus noster licet","la","textual","B: *appetitus noster quiuis licet* (the added word looks like *quiuis*, but the reading is not quite clear).","probable")
n("n1036","s008-05","Quedam sunt ad intendendum","la","textual","B: *Quedam sunt ad actum intendendum*, matching *ad actum remittendum* just before.","certain")
upd("n223",append="B: *et si non est virtus est tamen virtut(e)* (the ending is abbreviated), without *cum*.")
n("n1037","s008-06","magis amatiui","la","textual","B: *magis amati* ('more loved'), evidently a slip for A's *amatiui* ('more given to loving').","certain")
upd("n225",text="A prints *intellerit*; B prints *intellexerit* in full, which confirms the emendation. The subject is presumably Augustine (the *auctoritas* of the opening argument).",status="certain")
upd("n230",text="Supplying the subject. The verb is *intellexerit*, as in B (A misprints *intellerit*).")
